WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.556
CORS is a browser security mechanism that
controls which browser-based origins are

2
00:00:07.636 --> 00:00:12.704
allowed to make cross-origin requests
under browser rules.

3
00:00:12.784 --> 00:00:17.952
It is not general API authentication, and
it is not a firewall.

4
00:00:18.032 --> 00:00:24.194
A permissive CORS policy can expose
browser clients to unnecessary risk,

5
00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:27.951
especially when credentials are involved.

6
00:00:28.031 --> 00:00:33.397
A durable design starts by deciding which
clients are browsers,

7
00:00:33.477 --> 00:00:40.335
which are machine clients, and which
origins actually need cross-origin access.

8
00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:44.986
Credentialed browser requests require
special care.

9
00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:50.532
Cookies, bearer tokens in browser storage,
same-site behavior,

10
00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:54.985
and CSRF concepts all influence the
security model.

11
00:00:55.065 --> 00:00:59.139
If browser credentials are sent
automatically,

12
00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:06.573
the application should consider whether
state-changing actions need CSRF protection

13
00:01:06.653 --> 00:01:10.132
or another clear user-intent mechanism.

14
00:01:10.212 --> 00:01:13.292
If tokens are handled by JavaScript,

15
00:01:13.372 --> 00:01:18.341
the team should think carefully about
exposure, lifetime,

16
00:01:18.421 --> 00:01:21.204
and how response data is limited.

17
00:01:21.284 --> 00:01:28.141
Response models and response filtering are
important FastAPI features when used

18
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:29.613
intentionally.

19
00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:36.053
They can help ensure that internal fields,
secrets, administrative flags,

20
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:42.891
or unrelated model data are not returned
simply because an object contains them.

21
00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:46.449
Data minimization is a security control.

22
00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:53.188
The API should return what the intended
client needs for the current operation,

23
00:01:53.268 --> 00:01:58.038
not every field that was convenient to
load from storage.

24
00:01:58.118 --> 00:02:03.882
Response safety also includes redirects,
error messages, headers,

25
00:02:03.962 --> 00:02:06.446
and documentation exposure.

26
00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:12.887
Redirect targets should be constrained so
clients are not sent to untrusted

27
00:02:12.967 --> 00:02:16.346
destinations based on unchecked input.

28
00:02:16.426 --> 00:02:21.693
Error messages should be useful without
revealing internals.

29
00:02:21.773 --> 00:02:27.835
Security headers can add defense in depth
for browser-facing services.

30
00:02:27.915 --> 00:02:33.679
Documentation should match the expected
audience and environment.

31
00:02:33.759 --> 00:02:40.020
The response is part of the trust
boundary, not just the end of the function.
